WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.430 --> 00:00:04.828
One of my favorite machine learning algorithms is

00:00:04.829 --> 00:00:08.227
Generative Adversarial Networks, or GAN.

00:00:09.050 --> 00:00:12.515
It pits two AI models off against each other,

00:00:12.515 --> 00:00:15.980
hence the "adversarial" part.

00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:19.370
Now, most machine learning models

00:00:19.370 --> 00:00:21.920
are used to generate a prediction.

00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:25.520
So we start with some input training data.

00:00:27.070 --> 00:00:30.508
And we feed that into our model.

00:00:31.430 --> 00:00:34.250
A model then makes a prediction

00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:37.070
in the form of output.

00:00:39.020 --> 00:00:41.994
And we can compare the predicted output

00:00:42.046 --> 00:00:44.274
with the expected output

00:00:44.325 --> 00:00:46.836
from the training data set.

00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:50.670
And then based upon that expected output

00:00:50.721 --> 00:00:52.805
and the actual predicted output,

00:00:52.806 --> 00:00:55.826
we can figure out how we should update our model

00:00:55.877 --> 00:00:58.869
to create better outputs.

00:01:05.410 --> 00:01:09.454
That is an example of supervised learning.

00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:14.221
A GAN is an example of unsupervised learning,

00:01:14.271 --> 00:01:16.596
it effectively supervises itself,

00:01:16.596 --> 00:01:19.377
and it consists of two submodels.

00:01:20.291 --> 00:01:26.120
So we have a generator submodel.

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:34.796
And we have a discriminator submodel.

00:01:39.210 --> 00:01:43.032
Now, the generator's job is to create

00:01:43.033 --> 00:01:48.629
fake input or fake samples.

00:01:53.520 --> 00:02:00.190
And the discriminator's job is to take a given sample

00:02:00.190 --> 00:02:03.525
and figure out if it is a fake sample

00:02:03.525 --> 00:02:06.860
or if it's a real sample from the domain.

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:17.099
And therein lies the adversarial nature of this.

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:19.905
We have a generator creating fake samples

00:02:19.905 --> 00:02:22.710
and sending them to a discriminator.

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:25.829
The discriminator is taking a look at a given sample and figuring out,

00:02:25.830 --> 00:02:28.559
"Is this a fake sample from the generator?

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:35.129
Or is this a real sample from the domain set?"

00:02:35.130 --> 00:02:41.039
Now, this sort of scenario is often applied in image generation.

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:46.092
There are images all over the internet of generators

00:02:46.092 --> 00:02:48.770
that have been used to create fake 3D models,

00:02:48.820 --> 00:02:52.236
fake faces, fake cats and so forth.

00:02:53.341 --> 00:02:57.248
So this really works by the generator iterating through

00:02:57.298 --> 00:03:00.619
a number of different cycles of creating samples,

00:03:00.719 --> 00:03:02.999
updating its model and so forth

00:03:03.099 --> 00:03:06.122
until it can create a sample that is so convincing

00:03:06.272 --> 00:03:13.016
that it can fool a discriminator and also fool us humans as well.

00:03:14.330 --> 00:03:17.720
So let's let's take an example

00:03:17.720 --> 00:03:21.110
of how this works with, let's say, a flower.

00:03:21.621 --> 00:03:28.533
So we are going to train a generator to create really convincing fake flowers,

00:03:28.633 --> 00:03:32.400
and the way that we start by doing this is we need to, first of all,

00:03:32.507 --> 00:03:37.060
train our discriminator model to recognize what a picture of a flower looks like.

00:03:37.460 --> 00:03:40.283
So our domain is lots of pictures of flowers,

00:03:40.334 --> 00:03:43.754
and we will be feeding this into the discriminator model

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:45.786
and telling it to look at all of the attributes

00:03:45.887 --> 00:03:47.880
that make up those flower images.

00:03:48.500 --> 00:03:50.359
Take a look at the colors, the shading,

00:03:50.360 --> 00:03:52.219
the shapes and so forth.

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:56.495
And when our discriminator gets good at recognizing real flowers,

00:03:56.495 --> 00:03:59.990
then we'll feed in some shapes that are not flowers at all.

00:04:00.380 --> 00:04:04.137
And make sure that it can discriminate those as being not-flowers.

00:04:05.511 --> 00:04:08.891
Now, this whole time our generator here was frozen,

00:04:08.991 --> 00:04:10.072
it wasn't doing anything.

00:04:10.220 --> 00:04:14.840
But we're our discriminator gets good enough at recognizing things from our domain,

00:04:14.891 --> 00:04:20.951
then we apply our generator to start creating fake versions of those things.

00:04:21.320 --> 00:04:26.045
So a generator is going to take a random input vector

00:04:26.096 --> 00:04:33.121
and it is going to use that to create its own fake flower.

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:37.052
Now, this fake flower image is sent to the discriminator,

00:04:37.205 --> 00:04:40.175
and now the discriminator has a decision to make:

00:04:40.176 --> 00:04:44.688
is that image of a flower the real thing from the domain,

00:04:44.837 --> 00:04:49.149
or is it a fake from the generator?

00:04:50.580 --> 00:04:56.730
Now, the answer is revealed to both the generator and the discriminator.

00:04:56.970 --> 00:05:01.445
The flower was fake and based upon that,

00:05:01.496 --> 00:05:05.459
the generator and discriminator will change their behavior.

00:05:06.120 --> 00:05:09.899
This is a zero sum game, there's always a winner and a loser.

00:05:10.260 --> 00:05:13.649
The winner gets to remain blissfully unchanged.

00:05:13.650 --> 00:05:15.128
Their model doesn't change at all,

00:05:15.180 --> 00:05:17.497
whereas the loser has to update their model.

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:22.566
So if the discriminator successfully spotted that this flower was a fake image,

00:05:22.666 --> 00:05:26.643
then lead discriminator remains unchanged.

00:05:26.700 --> 00:05:30.359
But the generator will need to change its model to generate better fakes.

00:05:31.110 --> 00:05:32.879
Whereas if the reverse is true

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:36.350
and the generator is creating something that fools the discriminator,

00:05:36.403 --> 00:05:39.523
the discriminator model will need to be updated itself

00:05:39.524 --> 00:05:46.831
in order to better be able to tell where we have a fake sample coming in,

00:05:46.861 --> 00:05:48.736
so it's fooled less easily.

00:05:49.640 --> 00:05:52.513
And that's basically how these things work,

00:05:52.613 --> 00:05:55.289
and we go through many, many iterations of this

00:05:55.290 --> 00:06:02.056
until the generator gets so good that the discriminator can no longer pick out its fakes.

00:06:02.480 --> 00:06:07.310
And there we have built a very successful generator to do whatever it is we wanted it to do.

00:06:09.080 --> 00:06:18.384
Now, often in terms of images, the generator and the discriminator implemented as CNNs.

00:06:18.830 --> 00:06:21.407
These are Convolutional Neural Networks.

00:06:21.860 --> 00:06:26.971
CNN's are a great way of recognizing patterns in image data

00:06:27.021 --> 00:06:31.282
and entering into sort of the area of object identification.

00:06:31.700 --> 00:06:34.556
We have a whole separate video on CNNs, but they're a great way

00:06:34.656 --> 00:06:39.279
to really implement the generator and discriminator in this scenario.

00:06:40.011 --> 00:06:46.977
But the  whole process of a GAN, isn't just to create really good fake flowers

00:06:46.977 --> 00:06:49.128
or fake cat images for the internet.

00:06:49.631 --> 00:06:51.587
You can apply it to all sorts of use cases.

00:06:51.920 --> 00:06:55.579
So take, for example, video frame prediction.

00:06:57.190 --> 00:07:01.726
If we fit in a particular frame of video from a camera,

00:07:01.728 --> 00:07:06.982
we can use a GAN to predict what the next frame in this sequence will look like.

00:07:07.570 --> 00:07:11.641
This is a great way to be able to predict what's going to happen in the immediate future

00:07:11.792 --> 00:07:15.194
and might be used, for example, in a surveillance system.

00:07:15.940 --> 00:07:18.609
If we can figure out what is likely to happen next,

00:07:18.610 --> 00:07:21.279
we can take some action based upon that.

00:07:21.730 --> 00:07:25.410
There's also other things you can do, like image enhancement.

00:07:25.422 --> 00:07:28.351
So if we have a kind of a low resolution image,

00:07:28.451 --> 00:07:32.912
we can use a GAN to create a much higher resolution version of the image

00:07:33.012 --> 00:07:35.815
by figuring out what each individual pixel is

00:07:35.816 --> 00:07:38.619
and then creating a higher resolution version of that.

00:07:39.340 --> 00:07:42.729
And we can even go as far as using this for things that are not related

00:07:42.730 --> 00:07:45.344
to images at all, like encryption.

00:07:45.522 --> 00:07:49.422
But we can create a secure encryption algorithm that can be decrypted and encrypted

00:07:49.423 --> 00:07:53.190
by the sender and receiver, but cannot be easily intercepted,

00:07:53.241 --> 00:07:58.154
again by going through these GAN iterations to create a really good generator.

00:07:59.290 --> 00:08:00.450
So that's GAN.

00:08:00.791 --> 00:08:02.561
It's the battle of the bots

00:08:02.561 --> 00:08:06.687
where you can take your young, impressionable and unchanged generator

00:08:06.687 --> 00:08:10.813
and turn it into a master of forgery.

00:08:11.380 --> 00:08:13.870
If you have any questions, please drop us a line below.

00:08:13.870 --> 00:08:18.550
